diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7e1cc23
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,175 @@
+## Chat bots
+
+Bots are `User` records with `role: :bot` (see `User::Bot`). Because they're users,
+they're room members, own messages, show up in room member pickers and trigger
+broadcasts and push notifications with no special casing.
+
+### Data model
+
+- `users.bot_token` – random secret (24 alphanumeric chars), unique index. Cleared
+  when the bot is deleted, so the old code stops working right away.
+- `users.creator_id` – who created the bot. `User#bots` lists them;
+  `can_administer?(bot)` works because the bot has a `creator`.
+- `users.password_digest` can be null: bots have no password or email address,
+  so they can't sign in.
+- The **bot code** is `"<id>-<bot_token>"` (`User#bot_code`).
+  `User.authenticate_bot(code)` looks the bot up by id among active bots and
+  checks the token with `secure_compare`.
+
+### Managing bots (UI)
+
+- `/account/edit` → bot icon ("Set up chat bots") → `/bots` (`BotsController`).
+- Members see the bots they created; administrators see every bot. No pagination.
+  Bots outside your scope return 404.
+- Delete is a soft delete: `User#deactivate_bot` clears the token and calls
+  `deactivate`. The bot's past messages stay up.
+- `PATCH /bots/:id/code` (`Bots::CodesController`) generates a new bot code.
+- Bot codes are secrets. The index and edit pages send `Cache-Control: no-store`
+  and turbo-cache `no-cache`. The `bot_key` route param is filtered from logs by the
+  `:_key` filter. Bot codes are only rendered on `/bots`.
+
+### Posting API
+
+`POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages` → `Messages::ByBotsController#create`
+
+- Authentication: `allow_bot_access` (in `Authentication`) skips the session
+  and CSRF checks and prepends `require_bot_authentication`. That sets
+  `Current.user` to the bot, or returns `401`.
+- The room is looked up through the bot's memberships (`RoomScoped`). An unknown
+  room, or one the bot isn't in, returns `404`.
+- Body: the raw request body (`request.raw_post`, UTF-8, stripped). For
+  `multipart/form-data`, use `attachment` (a file) and an optional `body` field.
+- Blank body and no attachment → `422`. Success → `201` with
+  `Location: /messages/:id`.
+
+### Where bots are excluded
+
+- The account users list and role/deactivate actions (`User.without_bots`).
+- Session transfer links (so nobody can get a browser session as a bot).
+- Direct-message placeholders in the sidebar.
+
+### Webhooks (bots that answer)
+
+- `users.webhook_url` (nullable string). It's set in the bot form ("Webhook URL", optional,
+  `type=url`). The value is stripped, and a blank value is stored as `nil`. So saving the
+  form with the field empty removes the webhook. Only `http(s)://` URLs with a host pass
+  validation. `deactivate_bot` clears it as well.
+- Trigger: `Message` `after_create_commit` → `Room#receive` → `deliver_webhooks_later`.
+  Every active bot that is mentioned (`Message#mentionees`, so it must be a room member)
+  and has a webhook gets a `Bot::WebhookJob`. The only work done inline is enqueuing the job,
+  so the person posting never waits on the bot.
+  - Messages *created by bots* never trigger webhooks, so bots can't loop by replying to each
+    other. A bot mentioning itself doesn't trigger it either.
+  - Only new messages count. Edits don't trigger the webhook.
+- Delivery: `User::Bot::Webhook#deliver` (from `User#deliver_webhook`). It sends a `POST` with
+  `Content-Type: application/json` and a 7s open/read/write timeout. The host is resolved
+  through `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard` and that IP is pinned (SSRF: the reply gets
+  posted to the room, so internal addresses must be unreachable). Redirects aren't followed.
+  Network errors and timeouts are logged and dropped. The job never retries, since a
+  webhook call can have side effects.
+- Payload (`User::Bot::Webhook#payload_for`):
+  `{ event: "mention", author: {id, name}, room: {id, name, reply_to}, message: {id, body: {text, html}, link} }`
+  - `reply_to` = `room_bot_messages_path(room, bot.bot_code)`, the bot's own posting URL.
+  - `body.text` is the plain text with the bot's own mention attachments removed. Other
+    mentions stay in as `@Name`. Runs of blanks are collapsed and the text is stripped.
+  - `body.html` is `message.body.to_s` (rendered rich text, `<div class="trix-content">…`).
+  - `link` = `room_at_message_path(room, message)` (`/rooms/:id/@:message_id`). All paths
+    are relative.
+- Reply: a `200` with `text/plain` or `text/html` is posted to the room as the bot, and
+  broadcast. Plain text is HTML-escaped, with newlines turned into `<br>`. HTML goes through
+  the usual Action Text sanitizing. Bodies over 1 MB, blank bodies, `204`, other statuses and
+  other content types post nothing. The bot can always answer later through `reply_to`.
+- Autocomplete: bots are room members, so `Autocompletable::UsersController` (room scope)
+  already offers them for @-mentions.
+- Tests stub `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard.resolve` (the sandbox has no DNS) and
+  WebMock the webhook URL.
+
+### Room membership (opt-in open rooms)
+
+- People join every open room (`User` `after_create_commit :grant_membership_to_open_rooms,
+  unless: :bot?`). Bots don't, unless `users.joins_all_open_rooms` (boolean, default
+  `false`) is on. It's the bot form switch "Add to all open rooms" (a real checkbox inside
+  a `.switch` label).
+- `User.joining_open_rooms` = people + bots with the flag. `Rooms::Open#grant_access_to_all_users`
+  (on create, and when a closed room becomes open) grants to `User.active.joining_open_rooms`.
+- Turning the flag on (`after_save_commit :join_all_open_rooms`, also on create) adds the bot
+  to every existing open room. Turning it off removes nothing.
+- Otherwise a person picks the rooms: the closed room member picker lists bots like anyone else.
+  The open room form only lists bots that are (or will be) in the room
+  (`Rooms::OpensController#members_once_open`).
+
+### `joined` event
+
+- Every membership is created through `Membership.grant(rows)` (`Membership::Announceable`).
+  Don't call `Membership.insert_all` directly, or bots won't hear about it. `Room#memberships.grant_to`
+  and `User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms` already use it.
+- `grant` runs `insert_all(..., returning: :id)`. Existing rows are skipped by the unique
+  `(room_id, user_id)` index and return nothing, so only memberships that were really created
+  count. That's what makes `joined` fire exactly once per room, even when the switch is toggled
+  or a room is re-saved. A bot that is removed and added again gets a new `joined`.
+- For new memberships of active bots with a webhook in non-direct rooms, a
+  `Bot::JoinedWebhookJob(bot, room)` is enqueued in `ActiveRecord.after_all_transactions_commit`
+  (grants often run inside `Room.create_for`'s transaction).
+- `User::Bot::Webhook#deliver_joined(room)` checks the bot is still in the room (and that it isn't
+  direct), then POSTs `{ event: "joined", room: { id, name, reply_to } }`. The reply rules are
+  the same as for `mention` (shared `deliver_to`). The reply is posted into that room.
+
+### Bot limit per member
+
+- Account setting `bots_per_member` (in `accounts.settings` JSON via `has_json`, default
+  `Account::DEFAULT_BOTS_PER_MEMBER` = 5; `Account#bots_per_member`). Accounts saved before
+  the setting existed get the default automatically (`has_json` merges defaults on read).
+  It must be an integer ≥ 0 (`0` means members can't create bots at all). Invalid values
+  are refused and `AccountsController#update` redirects back with a flash alert.
+- Edited on `/account/edit` by administrators only: "Bots per member" (number field, submits
+  on change, like the other settings forms there).
+- `User#bot_limit_reached?` – false for administrators; otherwise
+  `bots.active.count >= bot_limit`. Deleted (deactivated) bots don't count.
+- Enforced in three places:
+  - `/bots` hides "Add a chat bot" and shows "You’ve reached the limit…" instead.
+  - `BotsController` `new`/`create` return `403` (`ensure_within_bot_limit`).
+  - Model backstop: `User::Bot` validates `creator_must_be_within_bot_limit` on create
+    (only when the bot has a creator), so `User.create_bot!` raises `RecordInvalid` too.
+- Lowering the limit never touches existing bots: it's only checked on create, so editing,
+  resetting codes and deleting bots over the limit still work.
+
+### Suspending misbehaving bots
+
+`User::Bot::Suspendable` (included by `User::Bot`). The limits are constants, not settings:
+`FLOOD_LIMIT` 10 messages per `FLOOD_WINDOW` 10s, `COOLDOWN` 30s, `COOLDOWN_ATTEMPTS_LIMIT` 5,
+`WEBHOOK_FAILURES_LIMIT` 10.
+
+- Columns on `users`: `suspended_at`, `suspension_reason` (string enum `flooding` /
+  `failing_webhook`, prefix `suspended_for_`), `cooldown_ends_at`, `cooldown_attempts`,
+  `webhook_failures`. Scopes `suspended` / `not_suspended`.
+- **Flooding**: `bot.post_within_limits { create the message }` returns `:allowed`,
+  `:cooling_down` or `:suspended`, and only yields on `:allowed`. Every post by a bot must go
+  through it: `Messages::ByBotsController#create` and webhook replies
+  (`User::Bot::Webhook#post_reply_to`).
+  - The rate is counted from the bot's own messages (`messages.created_at` within the last
+    10s, sliding window), so API posts and webhook replies share the budget with no extra
+    bookkeeping. Rejected posts aren't messages, so they don't count.
+  - With 10 messages already in the window, the next post is rejected and starts the cooldown
+    (`cooldown_ends_at`, `cooldown_attempts = 0`). Each post during the cooldown increments
+    `cooldown_attempts`; the 5th suspends (`:flooding`). That 5th attempt still answers `429`
+    (cooldown fields are kept on suspension so `Retry-After` stays sensible); later ones get `403`.
+  - Webhook replies during a cooldown are dropped, and count as attempts.
+  - The verdict is taken under `with_lock`, but the message is created *after* the lock:
+    `create_with_attachment!` analyzes the blob, which is only uploaded on commit, so it can't
+    run inside a transaction. Concurrent bursts may overshoot slightly; that's accepted.
+- API responses: `429 Too Many Requests` + `Retry-After` (whole seconds left, ≥ 1) while
+  cooling down. Suspended bots get `403` from `require_bot_authentication` (in
+  `Authentication`), before the room lookup. Blank posts still get `422` without being counted.
+- **Failing webhooks**: `User::Bot::Webhook#deliver_to` calls `bot.record_webhook_delivery(succeeded)`
+  for every delivery (mention and joined). Success = any `2xx` (`Net::HTTPSuccess`); other
+  statuses, timeouts, connection/SSL/DNS errors and `RestrictedHTTP` violations are failures.
+  10 in a row → suspended (`:failing_webhook`); a success resets `webhook_failures` to 0.
+- Suspended bots: `webhook?` is false (so no jobs are enqueued and queued jobs do nothing),
+  and the fan-out scopes in `Room#deliver_webhooks_later` / `Membership::Announceable` add
+  `not_suspended`. They stay `active`: rooms, messages, `/bots` listing and the bot limit
+  (`bots.active`) are unaffected.
+- UI: `bots/_bot` shows "Suspended" with the reason (`BotsHelper#bot_suspension_reason`) to
+  anyone who can see the bot. Administrators get a "Reactivate" button (`turbo_confirm`, i.e.
+  native `confirm()`) → `POST /bots/:bot_id/reactivation` (`Bots::ReactivationsController`,
+  `ensure_can_administer`, `403` for everyone else). `User#reactivate` clears the suspension,
+  the cooldown and the webhook failure count. Editing a suspended bot doesn't lift the suspension.
